# a-base-a-sortie-did-not-pay-for — plan 31 N5, the CONTROL half.
#
# The same base as `a-base-a-sortie-paid-for.keys` opened on the
# BASELINE alone — a first sortie, or one that came home empty.  ⚠ Two
# hundred points is two beacons (`@M065`) and one of them is this
# file's, because the pair holds the REMAINDER equal rather than the
# spend: both end the ferry on 100 points.
#
# ⚠⚠ **THE READING: 106.0 against its sibling's 230.0.**  Both bases
# beat the thirteen robots; the one with one tower finished barely
# better off than it started, and the one with three finished with more
# than double.
#
# ⚠⚠ **It drives every journey its sibling drives, carrying nothing on
# two of them.**  That is not padding: without it this file reaches its
# wave 18 ticks earlier and the pair would be measuring the recon window
# (`@X022`) as well as the towers, with no way to tell which paid.  ⚠ The
# player really makes the trip and really comes back empty, which is
# what an empty wallet looks like from the driving seat.

echo a base a sortie did not pay for: the same trips, one tower

do toggle_mode

do cycle_kind
click 0 0
marker 0 0 target

do cycle_kind
do cycle_kind
do cycle_kind
do rotate
do rotate
do rotate
click 11 0
marker 11 0 spawn 3

count markers 2 2
do toggle_mode

palette 5
drag -3 -2 12 -2
drag -3 -1 12 -1
drag -3 0 12 0
drag -3 1 12 1
drag -3 2 12 2
count painted 80 80

# The crew, one to each flank, standing within their own three hexes of
# BOTH tower sites on their side — `skill.loft::detect_radius` is 3
# untrained, and a crew member finds the nearest job inside it
# (`plans/29` O1).  Nothing else on this map is a job, so what they
# build is what the player ferried out and nothing else.
crew 5 -1
crew 5 1

# ── ⚠⚠ WHAT IS HELD EQUAL, AND WHY IT IS THE POINTS ─────────────
#
# ⚠⚠ **Both files end the ferry on a wallet of exactly 100.**  That is
# the control the whole reading rests on, and it was learned the hard
# way: `fall` plays until `wallet_broke`, and the wallet is what BUYS
# towers as well as what enemies drain — so a base that spends its
# budget on defence is "broke" before a robot arrives.  **The first
# version of this pair read 198 against 198**, both "fallen" on the tick
# the ferry ended, with twice the towers plainly on the map.
#
# ⚠ Holding the REMAINDER equal is what makes the difference the
# TOWERS.  Left unequal a richer base outlasts a poorer one by
# arithmetic — a hundred more points is a hundred more seconds of one
# nibbler — and the pair would price the carry's FACE VALUE rather than
# what it bought.  ⚠⚠ That is `@X292` in a fixture: **the wallet at zero
# is POVERTY and not an ending**, so a clock built on it has to say
# which kind of poverty it is timing.

park 0 0
wallet 200 200

# ⚠ The tower this base can afford, and its whole defence.
take player
drive 4 -1
tick 4
drop player
drive 0 0
tick 4

# ⚠⚠ The same two journeys with nothing to carry.  **No `take`**:
# `beacon_buy` refuses a purchase the wallet cannot cover and the `take`
# VERB fails loudly on a refusal, so the refusal is a VALUE in
# `tests/31_n1_the_budget.loft` and a FAULT here — *a script asserts
# intent*.  What this file asserts is the trip.
drive 4 1
tick 4
drive 0 0
tick 4

drive 6 -1
tick 5
drive 0 0
tick 5

wallet 100 100

# ── The crew raise them ─────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `numbers.json` § tower.construction_time_helper is 30 s a tower —
# three times a wall, *which is what makes a tower a decision rather
# than a reflex* (`build.loft`).
#
# ⚠⚠ **`count markers` is what says how many were RAISED, and
# `count orders 0 0` cannot**: a drop the game refuses leaves the beacon
# in the player's hands and creates no order at all, so the orders check
# passes VACUOUSLY on a base that built nothing.  Two of the base map's
# markers are the core and the spawn.
tick 140
count orders 0 0
count markers 3 3

# ── The waves — the authored list, as `an-undefended-base` runs it ──
#
# ⚠ The first five die to the towers in both files, so the count after
# the second wave reads 8 rather than 13.  That is the pair working: a
# defended base does not meet its second wave still carrying its first.
wave 5
count alive 5 5

tick 22
wave 8
count alive 8 13

# ── ⚠⚠ THE READING ──────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# ⚠ Played out rather than played until something breaks.  **Both bases
# beat this wave** — thirteen robots against a crew and at least one
# tower — so there is no `fall` here to read, and what separates them is
# what they have LEFT.
tick 300
count alive 0 0
wallet 105.5 106.5
ticks 486 490

# ── ⚠⚠ AND THE SWEEP IS THE FINDING ────────────────────────────
#
# The authored wave is one column of it.  Same pair, same everything,
# the wave list swept (`@M087`):
#
#     wave      1 tower              3 towers
#     5 + 8     stands, 106 pts      stands, 230 pts
#     10 + 16   FALLS at 229         stands, 354 pts
#     14 + 22   FALLS at 214         stands, 312 pts
#     20 + 30   FALLS at 214         stands, 257 pts
#     30 + 45   FALLS at 217         FALLS at 235
#     45 + 70   FALLS at 217         FALLS at 228
#     70 + 100  FALLS at 217         FALLS at 228
#
# ⚠⚠ **So the carry is worth three different things and the wave
# decides which**: at the authored wave it is +124 POINTS, from 26 to 50
# robots it is the difference between STANDING AND FALLING, and past 75
# it is +11 ticks and then nothing more.  ⚠ Both columns flatten —
# 214-217 and 228 — because the siege front is a WIDTH (`@M020`) and a
# big enough wave saturates it whatever is behind: `@M085`'s reading
# with a new subject, and the reason a flat pair must be priced against
# the capacity before it is believed.
